course: dictionary
lesson: 13486
title: Idolatry: worship and too much admiration
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Idolatry is a noun. It means worshipping an idol, which is a statue or object that people treat as a god. The Bible forbids it, and the Ten Commandments warn: «You shall have no other gods before me».
explain: «The people bowed down to a golden calf they had made.» The clue is bowed down. They gave the calf the honour that only God should get, so this is idolatry.
explain: The word has a wider meaning too: loving or admiring someone or something far too much, as if it were a god. «Tom's idolatry of his favourite footballer grew every week.» The clue is far too much, so here it means extreme admiration, not religion.
fact: Idolatry is an uncountable noun, so you say some idolatry, not an idolatry.
fact: An idol is a statue or object that people worship as a god.
fact: «Idolatry» means worshipping idols, which the Bible forbids.
fact: «Idolatry» can also mean loving or admiring someone far too much, as if they were a god.
fact: Phrases you may meet: «guilty of idolatry» and «the sin of idolatry».
exercise: «The people bowed down to a golden statue they had made.» What is idolatry in this sentence? | Singing a song at a party | Building a new school | *Worshipping an object as if it were a god | Painting a picture of the sea
exercise: «Tom's idolatry of his favourite footballer grew every week.» What does idolatry mean here? | Losing a game badly | *Admiring someone far too much, as if they were a god | Eating too many sweets | Walking to school in the rain
exercise: Which phrase means being at fault for worshipping idols? | Over the moon | Happy as a clam | *guilty of idolatry | Under the weather
test: Which is the best meaning of idolatry in a Bible lesson? | Repairing old church roofs | Learning to play the organ | *Worshipping idols, which the Bible forbids | Painting the church doors red
test: Ravi says idolatry is just a word for liking a film a lot. Is he right? | Yes, because it is a noun | No, it only means cooking | Yes, exactly the same | *Partly: it can mean admiring too much, but in Bible lessons it means worshipping idols
exam: «The crowd treated the singer like a god.» What does idolatry suggest here? | The crowd was lost | *The crowd admired the singer far too much, as if he were a god | The crowd was cold and bored | The crowd was hungry
